Title: EKPO
Credit: Sequences 1-7 Elevation Candidate
Draft date: August 13, 2026

===

# SEQUENCE 1 - THE LAST MORNING

FADE IN:

INT. SOLOMON'S HOUSE - PRE-DAWN

A kettle begins to whistle.

MALIK OKU, 36, appears from the pantry in funeral trousers and a white undershirt. He crosses the kitchen before the whistle reaches its full voice. Switches off the burner.

Checks the other three. Cold.

The house is ready for a man who will not come downstairs.

At the entrance: polished black shoes. A carved walking stick. Prayer beads looped over the handle.

In the living room: ACHEBE beside SOYINKA. An EFIK BIBLE with a repaired spine. Canadian immigration manuals decades out of date. A brass leopard darkened by fingerprints.

Family photographs arranged with care.

One frame hangs crooked.

The only one.

In the kitchen: three mustard jars in the refrigerator door. One thriving plant on the sill. Two dead ones beside it.

Malik pours hot water into a mug. Does not drink.

He wipes an already-clean section of counter.

Stops.

Looks toward the staircase.

The pale square of the landing. Solomon's bedroom door open above it.

The front door opens.

ADE OKU, 32, enters carrying his dress shoes. His tie hangs around his neck like a surrender flag.

He watches Malik wipe the same place again.

ADE
You know they sell those pre-cleaned.

MALIK
You said six-thirty.

ADE
I meant emotionally.

Malik checks his watch. 6:41.

Then the wall clock.

6:40.

ADE
Vancouver is running behind.

Malik watches the second hand. It hesitates at twelve. Moves. Hesitates again.

He takes the clock down. Removes the battery.

The ticking stops.

ADE
You won.

MALIK
It's broken.

ADE
That has never stopped you before.

Malik sets the clock face-down.

Three coffees wait beside the sink in a straight line.

Ade takes the one at the end.

Malik moves the remaining two closer together.

Ade notices.

He opens the refrigerator. Yellow mustard. Dijon. Grainy.

His thumb rests on the grainy lid.

He closes the door without taking anything.

MALIK
You need to change.

ADE
This is black.

MALIK
Those are sweatpants.

ADE
Grief has lowered its standards.

Malik removes a loose thread from Ade's coat sleeve.

Ade lets him.

The front door opens again.

KAI OKU, 25, enters with a digital camera against his chest and noise-cancelling headphones around his neck.

He stops just inside.

Listens.

Refrigerator hum. Water through old pipes. A delivery truck reversing two houses away. Malik folding a dish towel into exact thirds.

Kai looks at the face-down clock.

KAI
What happened to it?

MALIK
It loses time.

Kai turns it over. The battery rests beside it.

KAI
One second every minute.

ADE
Dad bought it at a gas station.

KAI
He hated losing time.

MALIK
Then he should've bought a better clock.

Malik lifts his mug. Still does not drink.

Near Solomon's chair sits an old CAMCORDER. Scratched casing. Masking tape underneath: SOLOMON.

Kai picks it up. Presses POWER.

Nothing.

He opens the battery compartment.

Empty.

KAI
Where's the battery?

MALIK
Leave it.

KAI
He kept them in the blue drawer.

Kai opens the narrow drawer beside the telephone.

Rubber bands. Receipts. Tiny screwdriver. Three dead pens.

No battery.

Kai searches beneath the receipts.

MALIK
Kai.

Kai closes the drawer.

Then opens it again.

ADE
Maybe it achieved freedom.

Kai keeps searching.

ADE
The battery. Not Dad.

No one laughs.

Ade looks down.

Kai removes the contents and arranges them on the counter. Rubber bands together. Pens parallel. Receipts by size.

One receipt is folded around something hard.

Kai opens it.

A small BRASS CARVING. Worn smooth at the edges. Animal at one angle. Mask at another.

KAI
What's this?

MALIK
Put it back.

KAI
You know what it is?

MALIK
No.

KAI
Then why was it wrapped?

MALIK
Not today.

Kai studies him.

Malik takes the carving. Rewraps it. Returns it to the drawer.

Kai crosses to the crooked photograph.

The three brothers as children beside Solomon. Malik rigid in a suit too large for him. Ade caught halfway through saying something. Kai small enough to be held.

Solomon looks directly at the camera.

Kai straightens the frame.

The ceiling light washes Solomon's face from the glass.

Kai tilts it crooked again.

Solomon returns.

KAI
He left it like that.

MALIK
How do you know?

Kai straightens it.

Solomon disappears in glare.

Tilts it back.

His face returns.

Ade watches.

ADE
Okay. That's annoying.

Malik lifts three garment bags.

MALIK
Get dressed.

On a lower shelf, an empty rectangle interrupts a line of dust.

Kai crouches.

KAI
Something's missing.

Malik checks the folder he brought. Funeral programme. Death certificate. Three printed schedules.

MALIK
We leave in twelve minutes.

KAI
Malik.

MALIK
I don't know.

That stops Kai.

Ade looks at the printed schedules.

ADE
You made an agenda for Dad's funeral.

MALIK
Someone had to.

ADE
Yeah.

Too sharp.

Ade softens.

ADE
Otherwise people might grieve out of order.

Malik aligns the folder with the counter's edge.

Ade takes the garment bags from him.

For one brief moment, Malik has nothing in his hands.

He looks toward the staircase.

The brothers follow his gaze.

Empty landing. Open bedroom door.

Malik turns away first.

On the dining table, the BRASS URN waits beside white flowers.

Smaller than the silence around it.

Malik lifts it. Tests the lid. Once. Again.

A faint thumbprint marks the polished brass.

He wipes it with his sleeve.

The print smears.

He wipes harder.

ADE
Malik.

Malik stops.

Ade holds out both hands.

ADE
Let me.

Malik looks at them.

MALIK
Take the flowers.

Ade leaves his hands there one beat longer.

Then takes the vase.

Kai switches off the kitchen light.

Dawn does the rest.

The brothers move toward the door: Ade with flowers and garment bags. Kai with his camera and funeral programmes. Malik with their father.

Before Kai leaves, he looks back at the crooked photograph.

He does not straighten it.

The front door closes.

CUT TO:

# SEQUENCE 2 - THE FUNERAL

EXT. MOUNTAIN VIEW CEMETERY - MEMORIAL LAWN - MORNING

Rain hangs in the air without committing.

A white canopy among cedars. Folding chairs. White flowers. Solomon's photograph. The brass urn at the center.

No grave.

A small Nigerian choir sings beside a portable speaker. The sound is beautiful until the speaker dries it into static.

Mourners fill the chairs. Black Canadian wool beside Nigerian formal wear. Church friends. Former patients. Neighbours.

The brothers stand in the front row.

Malik nearest the urn. Programme folded into a perfect rectangle.

Ade receives condolences with both hands. Each person gets the version of him they need.

Kai stands half a step behind. His camera hangs unused.

The OFFICIANT reads from the repaired Efik Bible.

OFFICIANT
May God receive Solomon Oku in peace,
and give comfort to the family who
remain.

Malik's eyes move to the order of service. Final prayer. Hymn. Family departure.

The choir leader raises one hand.

The microphone SHRIEKS.

Ordinary feedback. Metallic and sudden.

Everyone flinches.

Kai folds around the sound. One hand clamps over his right ear.

The choir, rain, murmuring crowd -- all of it collapses into a thin electrical ring.

His jaw tightens.

A copper taste reaches the back of his tongue.

The officiant's mouth keeps moving.

No words reach him.

Ade sees.

He steps close enough for Kai to feel him.

ADE
Kai.

Kai watches his lips.

ADE
Look at me.

Kai does.

Ade breathes slowly.

Kai matches him.

The speaker is switched off.

Sound returns in pieces. Shoes on wet grass. A cough into a handkerchief. The choir continuing without amplification.

The metallic taste fades.

Malik turns.

MALIK
You all right?

KAI
Yeah.

MALIK
Your ear?

KAI
Ringing.

Malik looks at the dead speaker. Then Kai.

At the rear of the canopy, a man waits beside a small travel bag.

BODE OKU, 64.

Rain darkens the shoulders of his coat. An airline tag still circles the bag handle.

He arrived too late to hide how far he came.

He removes his cap for the final prayer.

Malik sees him.

Something in Malik hardens before it gives way.

The prayer ends. People rise. Condolences begin moving toward the brothers.

Bode waits until the oldest church women have passed.

Then approaches.

BODE
Malik.

Malik takes his uncle's hand. Bode draws him into a brief embrace.

Ade. Then Kai.

Bode looks at the urn.

BODE
Was it peaceful?

Malik answers without looking away.

MALIK
Yes.

Ade lowers his eyes.

Kai studies Malik.

Bode waits.

Malik does not revise the answer.

BODE
Good.

ADE
You made it.

BODE
Your father spent seventeen years
telling me I was late.

ADE
Was he right?

BODE
Often enough.

Bode looks at Solomon's photograph.

BODE
At sixteen he missed the school bus
and ran behind it with his shoes in
his hands.

KAI
Why wasn't he wearing them?

BODE
Said they made him slow.

ADE
Dad said that?

Bode looks at Ade.

BODE
He was sixteen.

Ade laughs. One clean breath.

Even Malik almost does.

The FUNERAL DIRECTOR approaches.

FUNERAL DIRECTOR
Mr. Oku. Whenever your family is ready,
you may take the urn.

Malik moves first. Gathers the urn against his chest.

Ade reaches for the flowers.

FUNERAL DIRECTOR
The reception room is through the
south doors.

MALIK
Thank you.

Malik takes one step.

The urn slips less than an inch.

His entire body reacts.

Bode puts one hand beneath the base.

Malik does not release it.

Bode does not pull.

MALIK
I have it.

BODE
I know.

Malik's fingers whiten against the brass.

MALIK
I'm trying not to make another mistake
for him.

Bode keeps his hand beneath the urn.

BODE
Give me one side.

Malik looks at his uncle's hand.

Then shifts his grip.

Bode takes half the weight.

They carry Solomon together.

INT. MOUNTAIN VIEW CEMETERY - RECEPTION ROOM - LATER

Condensation clouds the windows. Coats steam on chair backs. Jollof rice. Meat pies. Fried plantain. Tea.

Solomon's urn rests where Malik can see it from anywhere in the room.

Mourners contradict one another over the man he was.

A woman tells Ade he has Solomon's laugh.

Ade thanks her.

For the rest of the reception, he smiles without showing his teeth.

Kai stops at a photograph on the memorial table.

Three young people outside a weathered house: SOLOMON. BODE. A GIRL between them.

Her face has been scratched away.

Not crossed out.

Cut from the emulsion with a sharp point, stroke after deliberate stroke.

Kai tilts the photograph.

The grooves catch fluorescent light.

Ade steps beside him.

His social smile disappears.

ADE
Somebody worked at that.

In the background, a brass object rests on the veranda wall. Too small to identify.

The same compact silhouette as the carving from Solomon's drawer.

Bode enters the edge of their silence.

He sees the photograph.

His face settles.

KAI
Who is she?

Bode looks at the damaged face.

BODE
My sister.

ADE
What's her name?

Bode looks toward two mourners close enough to hear.

BODE
Not here.

ADE
Did Dad do this?

BODE
I don't know.

Kai watches him.

Bode means it narrowly enough to matter.

Malik joins them.

MALIK
What is it?

Kai gives him the photograph.

Malik reads the damage before the people.

BODE
Solomon's lawyer will come tomorrow
morning.

MALIK
Why?

BODE
The will.

MALIK
You know what's in it.

BODE
Some of it.

Malik looks from the scratched face to the urn.

EXT. MOUNTAIN VIEW CEMETERY - PARKING AREA - AFTERNOON

The rain finally commits.

Bode and Malik carry the urn between them beneath one umbrella. Too small for both.

Ade walks ahead to open the car.

Kai follows with Bode's travel bag and the photograph protected inside his coat.

At the back door, Bode offers to take the urn completely.

Malik considers it.

MALIK
One side.

Bode nods.

Together, they place Solomon inside.

CUT TO:

# SEQUENCE 3 - THE WILL AND DECISION

INT. SOLOMON'S HOUSE - DINING ROOM - MORNING

The funeral flowers have begun to open.

The urn stands on the sideboard.

The damaged photograph lies face-up on the table beside the brass carving.

Kai has brought the two objects together.

MAYA PATEL, 48, Solomon's estate lawyer, has one slim file and a sealed envelope.

Bode sits apart from the brothers.

MAYA
The legal part is simple.

MALIK
That usually means the opposite.

MAYA
Your father left the Oku family house
and its adjoining land outside Calabar
to the three of you. One undivided gift.

ADE
And if one of us says no?

MAYA
It remains with the family there.
You owe nothing. You may refuse.

She places the sealed envelope between them.

MAYA
This is not legally binding. It's for
you.

Malik opens it.

One sheet in Solomon's tight handwriting.

He reads silently.

His jaw sets.

ADE
You have to use sound.

Malik gives him the page.

Ade reads.

ADE
"My sons. I need you to take me home.
All three. Bring my ashes to the river
boundary of the Oku land outside Calabar."

No farewell.

No signature.

MALIK
He's dead and still assigning seats.

MAYA
You may say no.

MALIK
Did he make the land conditional on it?

MAYA
No. The ashes are a request. The property
is a choice the three of you make together.
Your uncle agreed to guide you if you go.
That's all the will can usefully say.

She closes her file.

Malik walks her out.

INT. SOLOMON'S HOUSE - DINING ROOM - MOMENTS LATER

Ade holds Solomon's letter.

Kai turns the scratched photograph toward Bode.

KAI
This house. Calabar?

BODE
Yes.

Kai places the carving beside the tiny brass shape on the veranda wall.

Same broad shoulders. Same narrow center.

KAI
Same thing?

BODE
Looks like it.

KAI
Was it hers?

Bode studies the photograph.

BODE
I don't know.

KAI
You said she was your sister.

BODE
She was.

KAI
Her name?

A beat.

BODE
Mfon.

Kai looks at the scratched-away face again.

KAI
She died there?

BODE
She died in Calabar.

KAI
That's not what I asked.

BODE
It's what I can answer.

Malik returns. Takes Solomon's letter from Ade.

MALIK
We're not going.

KAI
You read one page.

MALIK
Exactly.

ADE
He said what he wanted.

MALIK
He gave us a location.

ADE
For Dad, that's practically a hug.

Malik does not smile.

Kai taps the photograph.

KAI
He kept this.

Then the carving.

KAI
He hid this.

Then Bode.

KAI
Uncle Bode won't answer where she died.

BODE
I answered what I know.

KAI
You answered around it.

Malik looks at Bode.

MALIK
Did Solomon know she was dead when he
left Nigeria?

BODE
Yes.

MALIK
Did he ever tell us she existed?

BODE
Not that I know.

Kai pushes the photograph toward Malik.

KAI
Then I want to see the house.

MALIK
Why?

KAI
Because every time I put two things
together, somebody tells me to stop.

That lands harder than eloquence would.

Malik turns to Bode.

MALIK
Should we go?

Bode considers the question.

BODE
If you go, I'll take you.

MALIK
That's not what I asked.

BODE
It's what I can promise.

Malik's jaw tightens.

Ade gets up. Opens the refrigerator.

Three mustards.

He takes out the grainy one. Turns it in his hand.

ADE
Kai's going.

MALIK
He hasn't booked anything.

ADE
Look at him.

Kai is already photographing the back of the damaged photograph.

ADE
If he goes alone, I'm going after him.

MALIK
That's stupid.

ADE
Correct.

Ade puts the mustard back.

ADE
So save me the second flight.

Malik looks at Kai.

KAI
I'm going.

Malik unfolds Solomon's letter. Smooths the crease Ade made.

MALIK
We go together.

Kai waits.

MALIK
We stay together.

ADE
That sounded threatening.

MALIK
It was a condition.

ADE
There he is.

A SEAT BELT CHIME carries us into --

# SEQUENCE 4 - ARRIVAL

INT. DOMESTIC AIRCRAFT - DESCENDING - AFTERNOON

Green gathers beneath the wing. Water catches the sky between broad seams of land.

The brothers look down at a place their father spent thirty years refusing to describe.

The urn remains in its padded case beneath Malik's seat. His shoes bracket it.

Across the aisle, Bode watches the ground rise.

WHEELS STRIKE RUNWAY.

EXT. MARGARET EKPO INTERNATIONAL AIRPORT - ARRIVALS - AFTERNOON

The doors open.

Heat arrives like a hand laid flat against the face.

Then sound. Car horns. Names called across traffic. Trolley wheels hitting broken pavement. An argument ending in laughter.

The brothers step out carrying Vancouver in the cut of their clothes.

Sweat finds them immediately.

ADE
Canada has been lying about weather.

A PORTER, twenties, moves toward their luggage.

PORTER
Oga, make I help.

He takes hold of Ade's suitcase before anyone answers.

Malik pulls it back.

MALIK
No. Leave it.

The porter keeps one hand on the handle.

PORTER
No worry. I go carry am.

Bode steps beside Malik. Does not raise his voice.

BODE
My brother, leave am. We no need help.

The porter releases it.

PORTER
No vex, sir.

BODE
No problem.

Five seconds.

Malik is still holding the suitcase too tightly.

Bode takes the trolley.

BODE
Come.

The brothers do.

EXT. CALABAR ROAD - BODE'S VEHICLE - LATER

The city passes in layers. Painted shopfronts. Church signs. School uniforms. Palm shadows across windshields. Red earth wherever pavement gives up.

Kai films through the window.

People.

A mechanic asleep beneath a lifted car. Two girls sharing earphones. A woman balancing bread on her head while texting.

Bode points toward a road disappearing into green.

BODE
Your father used to race bicycle there.

ADE
Dad had a bicycle?

BODE
Your father had a childhood.

KAI
We have not seen evidence.

BODE
He had legs too.

Ade laughs.

Malik looks out at the road.

For one moment, he tries to place his father on it.

Cannot.

The city thins. Walls give way to compounds. The air grows greener.

Bode turns onto a narrower road.

BODE
Home.

Tires leave asphalt for red earth.

CUT TO:

# SEQUENCE 5 - HOMECOMING

EXT. OKU FAMILY COMPOUND - LATE AFTERNOON

A broad family compound shaped by additions rather than one plan. Older central house with deep verandas. Newer concrete wing. Solar panels beside corrugated roof. Carved doors. Plastic water tanks.

Children's bicycles. Satellite dishes. Laundry moving in the heat.

Every part of the compound is in use.

The vehicle enters through an open gate.

People come out before the engine stops.

COUSIN EYO, 43, practical and quick, opens the rear hatch. Children crowd the brothers until one look from Eyo restores a lane.

Then AUNTIE ENO, early sixties, crosses the veranda.

She does not hurry.

Everyone else adjusts around her pace.

Bode steps forward.

BODE
Mọkọm, Eno.

ENO
Ekọm do.

Eno touches his cheek.

Then turns to the brothers.

Malik offers his hand.

ENO
That is all?

MALIK
Good afternoon, Aunty.

ENO
Mọkọm.

MALIK
Mọkọm, Aunty.

ENO
Better.

She ignores the hand and takes his face between both of hers. Turns it toward the light.

ENO
Solomon kept this forehead in Canada.

Malik does not know whether he has been welcomed or examined.

Eno releases him.

Ade opens his arms.

ADE
I prepared for a hug.

ENO
You people came from Canada and forgot
how to greet?

Ade lowers his arms.

ADE
Mọkọm, Aunty Eno.

ENO
This one still has mouth.

She pulls him into an embrace.

Ade closes his eyes inside it.

Only for a second.

Eno turns to Kai. He has already moved his camera out of the space between them.

KAI
Mọkọm, Aunty.

ENO
And this one came to inspect us.

KAI
I'm just looking.

ENO
Then greet first.

She touches his cheek once.

Then sees the padded black case in Malik's hand.

The compound quiets by degrees.

Bode removes his cap.

BODE
We brought him.

Eno approaches the case. Does not open it.

Lays her palm on the lid.

Ordinary gesture.

The grief is not.

ENO
You took your time, Solomon.

She removes her hand.

ENO
Bring him inside before the heat insults
him. The rest of you -- wash. You look
like the airport has eaten you.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - FRONT ROOM - MOMENTS LATER

Several generations without arranging themselves for visitors. Family portraits. School certificates. A charging station crowded with phones. Old carved cabinet beneath a flat-screen television.

The urn is placed on a sturdy side table.

Malik checks the surface. Solid. Level.

Eno watches him.

ENO
If that table survived this family, it
can hold your father.

Malik removes his hand.

Children peer through the doorway.

ENO
Give them air.

They disappear.

Not far.

At the end of the passage, a narrow door is secured by a faded RED CORD looped between its handle and a brass wall hook.

The only door in the house that does not stand open.

Kai notices.

Eno notices him noticing.

Eyo crosses the passage with folded bedding.

The door disappears behind ordinary work.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - DINING ROOM - DUSK

Food reaches the table before questions do. Afang soup. Fufu. Rice. Fried plantain. Grilled fish. Water sweating in glass bottles.

The brothers sit among relatives whose names arrive faster than memory can keep them.

Eno serves Malik until he says enough.

Then adds one more spoon.

MALIK
Aunty. Thank you. That's enough.

ENO
You are negotiating with soup.

ADE
He does it with eggs too.

ENO
Tomorrow I give him eggs.

Ade looks at his plate.

ADE
I should warn you, I can cook.

Eno studies him.

ENO
We will find out.

Eyo points his spoon at Bode.

EYO
Tell them about the mangoes.

BODE
There is nothing to tell.

ENO
Because you helped him.

ADE
Dad stole mangoes?

BODE
Borrowed.

ENO
From trees that did not belong to him.

BODE
The trees recovered.

EYO
He sold them back to the owner.

Malik laughs.

He looks as startled as anyone.

Covers it with water.

ADE
What else did he do?

ENO
At twelve he learned how to boil water.

BODE
Eleven.

ENO
Twelve. One month in the kitchen asking
why everybody was working without him.

Ade folds over laughing.

Kai smiles.

Malik looks toward the urn in the front room.

MALIK
He never told us any of this.

The laughter eases.

Eno takes a fish bone from a child's plate before the child finds it.

ENO
He left here young. Then he got busy
being your father.

Kai looks toward Bode.

KAI
What about Mfon?

The table does not stop.

It changes.

A spoon touches a plate more softly. Bode drinks water. Eyo reaches for plantain he does not need.

Eno looks at Kai.

ENO
You learned her name.

KAI
Yesterday.

ENO
Then yesterday was enough for yesterday.

MALIK
Aunty --

Eno takes Malik's plate before he can protect it. Adds more soup.

ENO
You came today. Eat today.

MALIK
We came because Dad asked us to take him
to the river.

ENO
And tomorrow Bode will take you.

KAI
And after?

ENO
After, you come back before dark.

Kai waits for more.

None comes.

Eno reaches for his plate.

Kai protects it.

KAI
I'm still eating.

ENO
Good.

She moves on.

Conversation starts again around them.

Through the doorway, the urn catches the room light.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - BROTHERS' ROOM - NIGHT

Three narrow beds. Three mosquito nets tied up for air. Suitcases open on the floor.

The room is hot enough to make sleep theoretical.

Ade lies on one bed, smiling at the ceiling.

ADE
Mangoes.

MALIK
Go to sleep.

ADE
He sold stolen mangoes back to the man.

KAI
Allegedly.

ADE
No. That's our father. I finally have
a complete fact.

Malik removes his watch. Sets it beside the bed.

MALIK
You have a story.

ADE
I'll take it.

Kai sits with the scratched photograph. The brass carving beside it.

MALIK
Put those away.

KAI
The object in the background is the same.

MALIK
We established that.

KAI
Nobody said her name until I did.

ADE
Now they have.

KAI
And everybody stopped chewing.

Malik looks at Bode's younger face in the photograph. Then at the missing one between the brothers.

MALIK
Tomorrow.

Kai looks at him.

MALIK
Not an answer. A time.

Kai wraps the photograph again.

Outside: family voices on the veranda. Cutlery being washed. A generator starting two compounds away.

INT. OKU FAMILY HOUSE - BREAKFAST AREA - MORNING

Morning arrives already loud.

Eggs. Fried plantain. Bread. Tea. Last night's Afang soup.

ENO
Emesiere.

BROTHERS
Emesiere nde.

Eno nods.

Malik eats the eggs.

She watches.

ENO
Well?

MALIK
They're very good.

ENO
Who won?

Malik surrenders one inch.

MALIK
The eggs.

Order restored.

Bode enters dressed for the land. Light shirt. Sturdy shoes. A folded map he does not need.

Malik notices the map.

BODE
We leave in twenty minutes.

ADE
Now he speaks your language.

BODE
We show you the boundaries first. Then
the river if the weather holds.

Malik looks toward the urn.

MALIK
Today?

BODE
You decide at the water.

CUT TO:

# SEQUENCE 6 - THE LAND AND THE SHRINE

EXT. OKU FAMILY COMPOUND - MORNING

Eyo secures water and food in a canvas pack.

The urn remains inside its padded case.

Malik lifts it.

Ade reaches for one handle.

Malik lets him.

Together, they carry Solomon through the gate.

Eno stands on the veranda.

ENO
Bring everybody back before dark.

ADE
No instruction for the dead?

ENO
He has ignored enough instructions.

Bode almost smiles.

The four men follow a red-earth path into the family land.

EXT. OKU FAMILY LAND - LATER

The compound disappears behind green.

The land has been worked, abandoned, reclaimed, worked again. Cassava plots. Palm stands. Old drainage cuts softened by rain.

New survey stakes beside boundary stones older than anyone present.

Bode stops at the first stone.

Three shallow cuts cross its face.

BODE
From here to the river bend. Then east
to the old road.

Malik studies the map.

MALIK
The title says forty-two hectares.

BODE
The title came later.

He points to a silk-cotton tree.

BODE
That was already old when your
grandfather was born.

ADE
Does the tree have paperwork?

BODE
Better memory.

They continue.

Kai photographs the stone.

The three cuts converge around a shallow hollow.

He takes the brass carving from his bag. Holds it beside the mark.

Angles differ.

Both hollows sit slightly off-center.

KAI
Uncle Bode.

Bode looks.

BODE
It marks Oku land.

KAI
And this?

BODE
It came from here.

KAI
Mfon's?

Bode looks at the carving.

BODE
Put it away.

Kai does.

EXT. OLD PALM TRACK - LATER

The path narrows beneath layered canopy. Heat becomes wet pressure.

The urn passes from Malik and Ade to Malik and Bode. Then to Ade alone for a short rise.

Each transfer careful.

No ceremony.

Practice.

Bode points to a tree bent low across the path.

BODE
Solomon planted that after our mother
said nothing useful would grow there.

ADE
Spite farming.

BODE
His best crop.

The tree is enormous now.

Malik touches its trunk as he passes.

Farther on, the path divides around a washout.

Bode stops them.

BODE
Stay where I can see you from here.

ADE
We have been very well behaved.

BODE
The slope isn't.

He points to the lower side of the path. Wet earth dropping beneath leaves.

BODE
Old washout. Ground gives before it
looks loose. No shortcuts.

Malik looks at Kai.

KAI
I heard him.

Bode takes the left fork.

The others follow.

EXT. BOUNDARY PATH - LATER

Boundary stones return at no regular distance. Each on the right side of the path.

A fallen limb has peeled vines from the slope to Kai's left.

Beneath them: an older stone carrying the same three cuts.

Facing downhill.

Kai raises his camera.

Ahead, Malik and Ade negotiate the urn over a fallen trunk. Bode watches their footing.

Kai steps off the path to frame the exposed stone.

EXT. SLOPE ABOVE THE RAVINE - CONTINUOUS

One careful step.

Then another.

Wet leaves shear beneath his shoe.

The slope gives.

Kai slides, camera clutched to his chest.

His free hand catches a root.

The root tears loose.

Kai drops into the ravine.

EXT. HIDDEN SHRINE - CONTINUOUS

He lands hard enough to empty his lungs.

Mud. Breath. Pain.

Kai checks the camera.

Unbroken.

One scraped palm.

The exposed boundary stone is not freestanding.

It is the upper corner of a SHRINE.

Stone and tree have grown into one body. Masonry rises from the ravine wall, softened by age. Roots enter old seams and emerge as carved lines. Some protect it. Others brace places where stone and earth have shifted against each other.

At its center: a narrow recess covered by a small slab of wood.

The wood is bound to the stone with faded RED THREAD.

Pale grooves circle it at different heights beneath the thread.

Earlier bindings.

Kai looks up the slope.

The family is no longer visible.

Movement above.

No words he can make out.

Kai takes the carving from his bag.

The roots describe the same three cuts around a hollow.

He holds the carving beside them.

Photographs both.

Kai approaches.

Touches the wood.

Dry beneath the damp air.

The red thread crosses itself in a practiced knot.

One loop can be loosened without cutting.

Kai works a fingertip beneath it.

The thread resists.

He stops.

Looks toward the slope.

Pulls again.

The old thread snaps.

A dry little sound.

The forest continues around him.

Insects. Birds. Leaves in the upper air.

Kai catches the wooden cover before it falls.

Behind it: a recess blackened by age.

Inside, a BRASS TALISMAN. Palm-sized. Green at the edges.

The same three converging lines.

Here, the hollow at their center is filled.

Kai photographs it where it sits.

Then removes it with two fingers.

The moment the brass clears the recess --

Insects.

Birds.

Leaves.

Gone.

The talisman pulls heat from Kai's fingers.

Copper floods the back of his tongue.

He swallows.

The shrine doubles.

Not blurred.

Two clean edges refusing to meet.

Kai shuts one eye.

They become one.

Opens it.

Another PALM beneath his.

Older.

Red thread cutting across both hands.

River water at his chest.

Somewhere below --

BRASS STRIKES STONE.

Kai drops to one knee.

His jaw locks against a cry.

The talisman remains in his fist.

The empty recess waits at eye level.

His free hand moves toward it.

Stops.

The ground answers with one DEEP BEAT.

Impact through root and stone.

Kai feels it in his teeth.

High above, leaves turn their pale undersides toward the ravine.

MALIK (O.S.)
Kai!

Kai raises the talisman toward the recess.

One inch from where it sat.

MALIK (O.S.)
Kai!

Kai's eyes settle.

The copper taste is already fading.

He looks at the talisman.

Then the recess.

Closes his hand.

Slips it into his camera bag.

At the base of the shrine, something moves.

A BLACK SPIDER emerges from the seam between root and stone.

Small. Unhurried.

It crosses the snapped red thread.

Stops on the broken loop.

BODE (O.S.)
Kai!

Bode's voice cracks on the name.

Kai looks toward the slope.

Then back at the shrine.

The spider follows the broken thread toward open ground.

CUT TO BLACK.

# END OF ACT I

# ACT II

# SEQUENCE 7 - THE RIVER BOUNDARY

FADE IN:

EXT. OKU FAMILY LAND - RIVER BOUNDARY - LATE AFTERNOON

The river moves beneath a low amber sky.

Broad. Brown. Unhurried.

Leaves and reflected light travel the current. Forest crowds both banks.

Bode removes his sandals beside exposed roots. Steps into the water to his ankles. Bends. Touches two fingers to the current.

Waits.

Looks back.

BODE
Face the current.

Malik removes his shoes.

Ade follows.

Kai is last. Mud dries across one knee. Camera bag high against his chest.

Malik looks at Kai's closed, scraped hand.

MALIK
You okay?

KAI
I fell.

MALIK
I can see that.

Kai meets his eyes.

KAI
I'm okay.

Malik does not believe the completeness of the answer.

But Bode is already waiting.

Kai enters the water.

Bode returns to the bank and steps back.

The BRASS URN rests in Malik's hands.

Smaller now than the journey built around it.

Malik opens it.

Looks down at the ash.

His fingers tighten around the rim.

Ade steps beside him. One hand on Malik's shoulder.

Malik looks to Kai.

Kai nods.

Malik tips the urn.

The first ash touches the river and spreads in pale veils. Some sinks. Some catches the current.

No music.

Water against their legs. Leaves shifting above. Insects working invisibly in the heat.

Malik tips farther.

Ade lowers his head.

Kai follows each grey strand until the river takes it.

A last dusting gathers along the lip.

Malik taps the base once.

One narrow ribbon of ash settles on the current.

The water moves beneath it.

The ash holds.

Kai sees it.

Then the ribbon opens. Separates. Disappears.

The river continues.

A bird calls beyond the trees.

Malik lowers the empty urn. Kneels. Rinses ash from his fingertips.

The grey leaves his skin.

When he stands, his shoulders sit lower.

Ade looks downstream.

Opens his mouth.

Closes it.

Kai shuts his eyes.

The brothers remain in the current.

Wind moves over the water. Sunlight breaks across it.

Then one insect stops.

A thin metallic chirr from the left bank cuts off mid-pulse.

Another disappears farther in.

Then another.

The change travels through the forest.

Life withdrawing from sound.

Ade opens his eyes.

The breeze weakens.

Leaves settle.

The river smooths around their legs.

A bird lands above the bank.

Does not call.

Malik's hand rises to the back of his neck.

He looks over his shoulder.

Only water.

Only forest.

His body locks.

One second.

His next breath does not arrive when he asks for it.

Then it comes.

He lowers his hand.

Ade turns toward the bank.

The beginning of a smile finds his face.

No joke follows.

He swallows instead.

Kai remains facing downstream.

His gaze fixes beyond the bend.

The trees seem closer.

He blinks.

They are where they were.

He closes one eye.

Nothing changes.

Opens it.

The distance is ordinary again.

Malik stops breathing.

Ade's unfinished smile disappears.

Kai's shoulders draw tight.

All at once.

Nothing touches them.

None of them turns.

The moment passes.

Wind crosses the river.

Insects restart unevenly.

The bird leaves the branch.

Water breaks around their ankles.

Malik steps first.

Ade follows.

Kai waits a beat before moving.

They leave the river separately, though they entered it together.

Bode watches from the bank.

Malik rubs the back of his neck.

Ade says nothing.

Kai keeps his eyes from the trees.

Bode looks past them toward the river.

Nothing there.

He hands Malik his shoes.

BODE
Let's go.

The brothers dress.

Bode starts up the path faster than he walked down.

Still short of a run.

After several steps --

BODE
Don't look back.

No one does.

Behind them, the current passes over the place where the ash held together.

Nothing holds there now.

# END OF SEQUENCE 7
